WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.550 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  anger on the streets of Haiti's capital  already struggling with a weak economy  these protesters are calling for an end  to high fuel prices and shortages during  violent clashes with demonstrators  police are accused of shooting and  killing a man amid protests in  port-au-prince close by one of his  relatives is overwhelmed with grief days  of protests have left the Caribbean  nation at a standstill roads have been  blocked and some schools banks and  government offices have closed their  doors our conditions are inhumane here  in Haiti the situation cannot continue  fuel suppliers say Haiti's government  owes them about a hundred million  dollars they have refused to make  deliveries until the debt is paid and a  corruption scandal has made things worse  auditors say the government embezzled  about two billion dollars from a fund  linked to a subsidized oil program for  Caribbean nations that money was meant  to improve infrastructure education and  health services president has plundered  the country's economy and become richer  while making us poor we demand that he  step down because if he continues the  system will not change over the past  year corruption scandals have led to  anti-government protests throughout  Haiti where the minimum wage is less  than $3 a day  despite the frustration president moiez  has refused to go earlier this month the  country's newly appointed prime minister  addressed the crisis the government will  focus on enforcing the justice system  and public security reinforcing the laws  of social equality in accelerating  economic growth in order to generate  employment protesters say the crisis is  about more than fuel prices they say the  demonstrations will continue until the  president and his inner circle step down  cod ciello pasado y on al Jazeera

